      "problem": "Solve the equation $13 = \\dfrac{6y+8}{2}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we clear the rational expression by multiplying both sides by $2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t13 &= \\dfrac{6y+8}{2} \\\n\t\t2 \\cdot 13 &= 2 \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac{6y+8}{2}\\right)  \\   \n\t\t2 \\cdot 13 &= \\cancel{2} \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac{6y+8}{\\cancel{2}}\\right) \\   \n\t\t26 &= 6y+8\n\t}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve the remaining equation using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t26 &= 6y+8 \\\n\t\t26-8 &= 6y+8-8 \\\n\t\t18 &= 6y \\\n\t\t\\dfrac{18}{6} &= \\dfrac{6y}{6} \\\n\t\t3 &= y\n\t}\n$$\n\nThus, $y=3.$",
